 | **ContextCov**: Bridging the Gap Between Developer Intent and Autonomous Agent Execution. [arXiv 2603.00822](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00822), v2 4 May 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | University of Washington | Compiles instruction files (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `copilot-instructions.md`) into four check modules: Tree-sitter queries for code patterns, PATH shims that intercept forbidden commands, architectural validators for layering, and a fourth that puts a model in the loop as judge. On SWE-bench Lite (12 repositories, 300 tasks) it reaches 88.3% constraint compliance against 67.0% prompt-only and 50.3% LLM reflection, at 3.4× lower feedback cost. Code: reSHARMA/ContextCov. | The closest prior art to loading rules as lessons that carry a check. ContextCov generates the checks from the prose and enforces at agent run time; okl keeps the check you wrote beside the lesson that says why, marks the lesson stale when the governed files move, re-runs the check on `okl reverify`, and blocks the push until it passes. The README's "instruction-file linters" bullet describes a narrower category than this; ContextCov is a constraint compiler, and the two are complementary: its three static modules generate candidate `--run` values; the model-judged one has no deterministic counterpart in okl. |
 | **TRACE**: Getting Better at Working With You: Compiling User Corrections into Runtime Enforcement for Coding Agents. [arXiv 2606.13174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13174), 11 Jun 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | University of Notre Dame, IBM Research, Tencent AI Lab | Memory alone does not force compliance: with Mem0 memory, 57.5% of applicable preference checks were still violated. Compiling corrections into runtime checks the agent must pass cut held-out violations from 100.0% to 37.6% in distribution and to 2.0% out of distribution on ClawArena, and from 100.0% to 60.5% on MemoryArena-derived tasks, while matching the strongest memory baseline on task pass. Code: YujunZhou/TRACE_exp and YujunZhou/tellonce. | The same thesis as a lesson with a `--run` check and a gate, measured on different benchmarks. TRACE mines corrections per user at session time; okl's checks are committed, re-run on demand after drift, and gated at push and in CI. Its "access–compliance gap" is the analogue of what okl's harness reports, not the same measure: TRACE counts violations of applicable user-preference checks, while [evals/REPORT.md](../evals/REPORT.md) §5 finding 7 counts reproduction of known defects on seeded tasks, 42% → 7% (68 of 163 unbriefed attempts against 11 of 164 briefed), and claims nothing about general code quality. |
 | **Phantom Guardrails**: When Self-Improving Agent Harnesses Fix Failures That Never Happened. [arXiv 2607.13083](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13083), 13 Jul 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | Carnegie Mellon University, Corespeed Inc., Georgia Tech, independent researchers | A suppression-rewarded proposer invented a guardrail for a rule violation that provably never occurred in 15/60 runs when the input carried a harmless rule-like pattern, against 0/60 on featureless input, across five proposer models. Three conditions must co-occur: a rule-shaped pattern, an open-ended rule set, and an instruction that presupposes failures. Under add-only acceptance the phantom is absorbing; warrant-aware acceptance excludes it. Instrument: the Counterfactual Fabrication Lab, deterministic, with a byte-exact oracle. | The failure mode okl's evidence rule exists to prevent: no stamp without a run, `found_by` on every seed record (a corpus convention the CLI does not yet enforce; see the open work below), and "reproduced" in the evals is a blind cross-model judge's verdict against a stated signal, with the receipt committed. The Stop hook's question ("did it surface a lesson worth keeping?") presupposes a lesson, which is their third condition. Their lab is the control condition for any auto-curation experiment. |
-| **DreamBench-SWE**: A Multi-Session Memory-Hygiene Benchmark for Software Agents. [arXiv 2608.20664](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20664), 21 Aug 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | Sarthak Singh, independent researcher | Later tasks depend on non-inferable evidence from earlier sessions, graded by hidden executable oracles. The primary contrast between a typed-plus-raw memory probe and verbatim event memory was null (95/180 against 89/180, p=.518). In the successor audit: no memory 21/180, verbatim event memory 82/180, the reference probe 83/180, a hosted Mem0 literal-storage configuration 97/180. In the original v2 fold, the aggregate executable hygiene diagnostics were StaleUseRate 0.200, RepeatedErrorRate 0.571, OverscopeRate 0.167 and IrrelevantImportRate 0.474. | Staleness is measured here as stale *use* by the agent. DreamBench-SWE does not report whether a memory system *detects* that a stored lesson has gone stale before it briefs; papers outside this reading do (#264). okl's drift is such a detector. REPORT §4ae measured its precision on this repository's history, and its recall is still open. The paper's own habit of narrowing its claims is the standard this documentation is written to. |
+| **DreamBench-SWE**: A Multi-Session Memory-Hygiene Benchmark for Software Agents. [arXiv 2608.20664](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20664), 21 Aug 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | Sarthak Singh, independent researcher | Later tasks depend on non-inferable evidence from earlier sessions, graded by hidden executable oracles. The primary contrast between a typed-plus-raw memory probe and verbatim event memory was null (95/180 against 89/180, p=.518). In the successor audit: no memory 21/180, verbatim event memory 82/180, the reference probe 83/180, a hosted Mem0 literal-storage configuration 97/180. In the original v2 fold, the aggregate executable hygiene diagnostics were StaleUseRate 0.200, RepeatedErrorRate 0.571, OverscopeRate 0.167 and IrrelevantImportRate 0.474. | Staleness is measured here as stale *use* by the agent. DreamBench-SWE does not report whether a memory system *detects* that a stored lesson has gone stale before it briefs; papers outside this reading do (#264). okl's drift is such a detector, though okl's main aim is to be the better memory store: its retrieval is meant to replace a hosted Mem0 configuration, not run beside one. REPORT §4ae measured drift's precision on this repository's history, and its recall is still open. The paper's own habit of narrowing its claims is the standard this documentation is written to. |
 | **Memory Transfer Learning**: How Memories are Transferred Across Domains in Coding Agents. [arXiv 2604.14004](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14004), 15 Apr 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | KAIST, New York University, DeepAuto.ai | A unified memory pool across heterogeneous coding domains improved average scores on 6 benchmarks by 3.7%. The gain comes from meta-knowledge such as validation routines, not task-specific code. Abstraction dictates transferability: Insight-format memories transferred best; raw Trajectories were the weakest format, negative on two of the six benchmarks, which the abstract states as often inducing negative transfer. Transfer scales with pool size and works across models. | A design implication rather than a test of okl: the paper did not evaluate `org` scope, `applies_to` or the Gate type. It argues for recording abstract rules and validation routines, which is what those three exist for, and against recording raw traces as lessons without abstracting them first. |
 | **ReasoningBank**: Scaling Agent Self-Evolving with Reasoning Memory. [arXiv 2509.25140](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25140), v2 16 Mar 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, Google Cloud AI Research, Yale University | Distils reasoning strategies from successful and failed experiences, judged by the agent itself without ground-truth labels. Its headline maxima, up to 20% relative improvement and up to 16% fewer interaction steps, come from its Table 1 (the WebArena experiment); it also evaluates Mind2Web and SWE-Bench-Verified. Code: google-research/reasoning-bank. | The unverified end of the line: admission by self-judgment. Its released code is the baseline for a verified-against-unverified comparison. |
 | **ACE**: Agentic Context Engineering: Evolving Contexts for Self-Improving Language Models. [arXiv 2510.04618](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04618), v3 29 Mar 2026, ICLR 2026 (pp. 1–2 read) | Stanford University, SambaNova Systems, UC Berkeley | Contexts as evolving playbooks updated by generation, reflection and curation. +10.6% on agents (AppWorld) and +8.6% on the finance benchmark, without labeled supervision, using execution feedback. Finds that concise summaries lose domain detail ("brevity bias") and that iterative rewriting erodes contexts ("context collapse"). Code: ace-agent/ace. | The playbook shape okl shares, with admission by reflection rather than by a check that ran. Its brevity-bias finding is a reason to measure the 12-record briefing limit rather than assume it. |
